      <h3>How the order describes the dispute</h3>

      <p>
        The order says: "This dispute involves Abebe Caesar Ventures, LLC
        ('the Company'), a business formed to pursue real estate development
        projects in Georgia."
      </p>

      <p>
        Three matters were before the court: the plaintiff's "Emergency
        Petition for Interlocutory Injunction", the plaintiff's "Emergency
        Motion for Interim Relief", and the defendants' "Motion to Stay and to
        Compel Arbitration".
      </p>

      <p>
        The order says: "All parties to this litigation agree that the
        operating agreement contains an arbitration clause allowing any of the
        parties to that agreement to send this dispute to arbitration."
      </p>

      <h3>What the court ordered</h3>

      <p>The order grants the injunction and instructs as follows.</p>

      <div class="document-list">
        <div class="document-row">
          <div>
            <strong>Transfer of the properties</strong>
            <p>
              "Defendants shall transfer whatever interest they hold in each
              of the post-closing properties to the Company within seven
              business days of the date of this order. Where the transfer of
              the property could result in a default under a loan agreement or
              otherwise jeopardize the Company's interest in the property, the
              parties shall work in good faith to find a suitable method of
              transfer, such as placing the properties in trust or escrow, as
              appropriate."
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="document-row">
          <div>
            <strong>Loan documents</strong>
            <p>
              "Within three business days of the date of this order,
              Defendants shall provide copies of all loan documents associated
              with any of the post-closing properties not yet transferred to
              the Company."
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="document-row">
          <div>
            <strong>Who speaks for the project at City Hall</strong>
            <p>
              The court authorizes a named consultant "to serve as the 1313
              Sylvan Project's representative before the City of Atlanta for
              the entitlement process to secure all necessary executive and
              legislative approvals including the Atlanta City Council." The
              order adds that such communications "shall be limited to
              project-related matters and shall not address the Parties'
              underlying legal dispute."
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="document-row">
          <div>
            <strong>Arbitration and the stay</strong>
            <p>
              "This matter shall remain stayed pending arbitration. This Court
              retains jurisdiction to approve the arbitration award, as well
              as to address ancillary matters, such as requests for interim
              relief or disputes arising under this order."
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="document-row">
          <div>
            <strong>Status report</strong>
            <p>
              "The parties shall file a joint status update with this Court in
              90 days stating whether either side has filed a request for
              arbitration. If no party has submitted this case to arbitration,
              the parties shall state their positions on whether this case
              should be dismissed without prejudice."
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>What the court found at this stage</h3>

      <p>
        These are findings on a request for a preliminary injunction. They
        are not a final ruling on who is right. The order leaves the dispute
        itself to arbitration.
      </p>

      <p>
        On the risk of injury: "The Company not holding title to each of the
        properties poses a tangible risk of injury, including that the 1313
        Sylvan Project could be delayed or derailed, that the Company may
        suffer reputational injury, or that title to the property may
        ultimately be unobtainable at the conclusion of arbitration."
      </p>

      <p>
        On the likely outcome: "this Court finds that there is a substantial
        likelihood that the Company will prevail in arbitration that Mr. Abebe
        breached the operating agreement. The operating agreement appears
        unambiguous that Mr. Abebe is required to transfer title to each of
        the post-closing properties."
      </p>

      <p>
        On harm to the defendant: "granting injunctive relief will not work
        any harm to Mr. Abebe, who, like the Company's other investors, stands
        to benefit if the 1313 Sylvan Project is completed."
      </p>

      <p>
        On the public interest: "injunctive relief will not harm the public,
        and potentially provides the public with a benefit by ensuring that
        the 1313 Sylvan rezoning process is not disrupted."
      </p>

      <h2>What Each Side Told the Court</h2>

      <p>
        These are the parties' positions as the order summarizes them. Each is
        a claim by a party. The order does not decide them.
      </p>

      <table class="report-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>The Company, as the order summarizes it</th>
            <th>Mr. Abebe, as the order summarizes it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>
              "The Company alleges that one of its business partners, Mekonnen
              Abebe, has violated certain contractual and fiduciary duties he
              owes under the Company's operating agreement."
            </td>
            <td>
              "Mr. Abebe denies those allegations and contends that the
              Company owes him money for past salary and other unreimbursed
              expenses."
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              "Plaintiff contends that Mr. Abebe has failed to transfer title
              to certain properties he was obligated to transfer to the
              Company under the operating agreement."
            </td>
            <td>
              The order says he asserted "that the Company had an unwritten
              agreement with him to pay off the entirety of the Company's debt
              prior to transfer."
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>
              "Plaintiff contends that his failure to transfer title puts a
              development project at risk that the Company is currently
              pursuing with the City of Atlanta".
            </td>
            <td>
              The defendants asked the court "to stay this case pending
              arbitration, or, in the alternative, to dismiss Plaintiff's
              complaint." The court granted the stay and denied the rest.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>What the Order Does Not Say</h2>

      <p>
        The order does not use the name "Renewal Development Group". It does
        not name the Company's "other investors". It gives no Georgia control
        number for the Company. It does not list the "post-closing properties"
        by address or parcel; it refers to "the 1313 Sylvan Project". It does
        not say whether any transfer was made afterward.
      </p>

